{"body": "One supported criticism concerns J’s sentence:\n\n> “The objection targets the stronger claim that the interval is exact, convex, or decision-sufficient.”\n\nThe antecedent is “P9’s compatibility objection.” But P9 itself separates the compatibility/correlation point from the downstream-policy criticism. It says:\n\n> “That gives an interval, but intervals lose correlations: booking A’s high alternative might be incompatible with booking B’s high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot. The snapshot does not name physical resources, so this incompatibility is invisible.”\n\nand then, as a distinct criticism:\n\n> “A criticism: exposing alternatives without a policy for resolving them pushes the problem downstream, where it may be resolved badly or inconsistently.”\n\nThe first criticism is conditional on a hidden cross-booking resource constraint. The second is not. Even with no hidden resource incompatibility at all, exposing unresolved alternatives without a resolution policy can still push the problem downstream. Conversely, a hidden incompatibility can make an interval loose or non-convex without making it decision-insufficient relative to some downstream threshold.\n\nA distinguishing case: suppose there is no cross-booking incompatibility, but a downstream consumer needs one availability number. The compatibility objection has no purchase, yet P9’s downstream-policy criticism still applies. Conversely, suppose two bookings have alternatives that are jointly incompatible, but the downstream decision only asks whether availability is positive. The compatibility objection refutes an exact/envelope reading of the interval, but the interval may still be decision-sufficient. So “decision-sufficient” is not simply a stronger version of the same bound/envelope claim targeted by the compatibility objection. It is an independent property relative to a decision context.\n\nJ later partly acknowledges this when it says:\n\n> “Decision commitment and resource model included: the objection expands into a downstream-policy criticism.”\n\nThat charitable reading can be made consistent with P9. But the earlier sentence, “the objection targets … decision-sufficient,” collapses the compatibility objection and the downstream-policy objection into one scale of “claim force.” That loses a distinction the operator question explicitly asks to preserve: “exposing alternatives and objecting to downstream resolution are disputed positions.” The defect is established for the literal reading of J’s sentence; under the more charitable reading, J is making an unmarked addition rather than accurately reporting what the compatibility objection targets.\n\nA second, more local defect also appears in J’s RSS grounding. J says:\n\n> “in RSS terms, an account can be represented as a viewpoint, a reading, a projection, and a set of accepted conjectures.”\n\nBut RSS says:\n\n> “An account is a viewpoint, a reading, and a set of accepted conjectures.”\n\nand defines projection separately:\n\n> “Projection: a function from a viewpoint and a reading to a single answer (for example, a single availability number). Projections are allowed but not privileged.”\n\nSo J adds projection to the account and then says:\n\n> “RSS’s projection primitive lets us ask what claim about that object is being criticized.”\n\nRSS’s projection primitive does not carry the bound/envelope/decision distinction. It maps a viewpoint and reading to a single answer. An interval [L,U] is not a single answer, and the difference between a bound claim, an envelope claim, and a decision claim is a difference in what is conjectured or committed about the interval, not a difference in the projection function. That work belongs to RSS’s conjecture/commitment vocabulary, not its projection primitive. If J means to extend RSS so that projections can output intervals or so that accounts include projections, that extension should be marked as an addition rathe